[8:48:02 AM] [semantic-release] [@semantic-release/commit-analyzer] › ℹ  Analyzing commit: feat(logger): hide RPC API keys in URLs sent to logz.io and sentry (#1257)

The external transport now scrubs API keys embedded in URLs from the
log payload before it leaves the process. Three URL formats this
codebase actually uses are handled:

- Alchemy:  alchemy.com/v2/<key>        -> .../v2/[REDACTED]
- dRPC:     ?dkey=<key>                 -> ?dkey=[REDACTED]
- Ankr:     rpc.ankr.com/<chain>/<key>  -> .../[REDACTED]

Redaction runs on a serialized JSON snapshot of the log payload built
inside ExternalLogger.log(), never on the live winston `info` object.
Winston shares that reference across all transports (console + external),
so in-place mutation would bleed into local console output, and some
libraries like ethers define own Error properties (e.g. `code` on a
TypeError from `invalid address`) as read-only which makes even a
no-op assignment throw. The serialized form is a plain object with
neither Error instances nor frozen properties, so `redactPayload`
can walk it safely.

- src/logger/redact.ts: redactUrlKeys() for individual strings,
  redactPayload() walks plain objects/arrays in place. String
  assignment is guarded with `if (redacted !== current)` so no-op
  writes are never attempted.
- src/logger/external.ts: scrub each of the two downstream paths
  (logzio, sentry) independently. For Sentry, construct a fresh
  Error with a redacted message/stack instead of mutating
  info.error. Both paths are wrapped in their own try/catch with
  a try/finally around the winston callback, so any serialization
  or transport failure is swallowed locally — broken logs must
  not tear down the app.
- test/unit/logger/redact.spec.ts: 13 tests covering each URL
  pattern, negative cases, nested structures, Error instances
  flowing through unchanged, a non-writable `code` property
  (mirroring the ethers TypeError shape), circular refs, and
  primitive inputs.
- test/unit/logger/external.spec.ts: the two Sentry assertions
  now check scalar fields on the serialized extras snapshot
  (level, message, errorMessage, userId) instead of deep-equalling
  the live log reference.
[8:48:02 AM] [semantic-release] [@semantic-release/commit-analyzer] › ℹ  The release type for the commit is minor
[8:48:02 AM] [semantic-release] [@semantic-release/commit-analyzer] › ℹ  Analyzing commit: fix(assets): route Citrea token balances through Blockscout v2 (#1261)

* fix(assets): route Citrea token balances through Blockscout v2

Alchemy's Enhanced API (`alchemy_getTokenBalances`) is disabled on Citrea
and returns `-32600 "EAPIs not enabled on specified network:
[CITREA_MAINNET]"`. `Web3Helper.getTokenBalances` swallows that into an
empty array, which is why every Citrea DAO in the UI shows no ERC20
assets — only the native cBTC balance row is ever written.

Citrea Blockscout also rejects the legacy Etherscan-compat path
`?module=account&action=addresstokenbalance` with HTTP 400, but its
modern REST v2 endpoint `/api/v2/addresses/{addr}/token-balances`
returns strictly-typed ERC-20 balances with full metadata.

Changes:

- `evmExplorerClient.getTokenBalances` branches on BLOCKSCOUT and
  dispatches to a new private `getBlockscoutV2TokenBalances` that hits
  the v2 REST endpoint, filters to ERC-20 only (skipping ERC-721 /
  ERC-1155 entries), and normalizes through the same
  `utils.parseTokenBalance` as the legacy path. Non-Blockscout explorers
  continue to use the Etherscan-compat route untouched.

- `Web3Provider.getTokenBalances` routes `citreaMainnet` to
  `evmExplorerClient.getTokenBalances(BLOCKSCOUT, …)` — same pattern
  the file already uses for `fetchContractCreation` and
  `fetchContractSourceCode`. The explorer path skips
  `handleAlchemyCrazyBalance` because Blockscout v2 returns already-parsed
  decimal strings; all other chains keep the Alchemy path verbatim.

- New unit-dep integration test hits real Citrea Blockscout, asserts the
  JUCY 5e18 deposit is returned with correct metadata, runtime ~430ms.

No code change needed for non-Citrea chains. This only affects networks
explicitly routed through `EvmExplorerEnum.BLOCKSCOUT` — currently just
Citrea — so Ethereum/Polygon/Base/Arbitrum/etc. continue to use
